-# First 0.3% Visual Benchmark
+# Restart milestone V1 — reference selection and tiny visual proof
 
-This document defines the visual benchmark for the first 0.3% of Game Store Sim.
+Status: V1a references presented; owner selection pending. V1b unstarted. See [reference board](../05-reference/presentation-choice-2026-09-07.md). This replaces the old nine-view benchmark. The filename is retained for existing links; no completion percentage is implied.
 
-The engine proof proved that Godot, macOS export, validation, basic item state, customer state, sale state, and save/load can work. It did not prove the game looks right.
+## V1a — choose the target
 
-The next production phase is visual-first. Do not expand gameplay breadth until this benchmark is signed off.
+Present at most three concrete reference candidates spanning illustrated 2D, angled/2.5D, and 3D. Include actual stills and a motion example where available, source attribution, intended gameplay framing, retail readability, production cost, and the hardest uncertainty. If motion evidence is unavailable, state that gap. Do not ask for an abstract style choice without showing the references.
 
-## Goal
+Record the owner's primary reference selection and exact feedback. Define the chosen camera, needed directions, target displayed size/window scale, and a single hardest visual requirement. This is the only owner decision needed before the sample. No art generation before selection.
 
-Build the visual scaffold for the opening store so future systems expand on a strong visual foundation instead of re-solving the look later.
+## V1b — one character, one repeatable motion test
 
-The target player reaction is:
+Build a separate, minimal engine scene only after V1a. Use a plain floor, one customer, and one simple occluder/fixture silhouette if needed. No store scenery or expanded simulation. The character walks a short route, stops, turns through the directions required by the chosen view, and repeats. Include camera motion only if the selected presentation needs it. Record exact launch command and controls.
 
-> This already feels like a warm early-2000s mall game shop, even before the full systems exist.
+For illustrated 2D, test illustrated character motion concretely: layered cutouts with AnimationPlayer are a candidate workflow, not a promised outcome. Isolate every moving limb and reconstruct hidden surfaces; inspect for duplicate silhouettes, gaps, drifting pivots, and foot sliding. For 2.5D additionally test depth, facing and occlusion. For 3D test the actual rigged character's turn/walk and stylization under representative lighting and camera distance. A capsule proves none of these.
 
-## Scope
+Start near 100 pixels displayed character height where appropriate; derive the final test size from the selected reference and framing. Inspect animation at real gameplay size, plus magnified diagnostic views. Record window size, viewport size, Retina scaling, displayed character height, filtering, and any mipmap/alpha settings. The small view must carry the quality judgment.
 
-This benchmark covers only the 0.0%-0.3% opening experience:
+## Required asset and evidence package
 
-- mall concourse
-- storefront
-- empty lease/sales floor
-- counter/register
-- receiving/backroom
-- starter shipment
-- starter shelves/racks
-- physical game cases and boxes
-- carried item view
-- first stocked shelf view
