-## 2. Non-Negotiable Direction
+Preparation is complete. The [three-reference board](05-reference/presentation-choice-2026-09-07.md) is presented: A Potion Craft illustrated 2D counter/shop panels, B Kardboard Kings illustrated 2.5D overview, C Winkeltje 3D overview. Owner selected B with the exact reply “B”. Krita handles editable artwork and cleanup; Godot handles running animation. Next production task: the bounded B visual proof; no art or sample is built in this reference-selection task.
 
-These are locked until intentionally changed through a decision doc:
+Then specify and run only the [tiny visual proof](01-design/visual-benchmark-first-0.3.md). Test the selected approach's hardest visual requirement before scenery. Do not preselect illustrated cutouts if a different approach wins. Space-opera RPG shows that illustrated character motion merits a concrete test; it supplies neither this game's style nor camera.
 
-- Setting: mall storefront.
-- View: first-person.
-- Initial platform: macOS.
-- Initial gameplay scope: 0.0%-0.3% first playable.
-- Tone: warm nostalgia, grounded retail, lightly idealized but not sterile.
-- IP policy: all brands, platforms, products, games, publishers, distributors, and store names are fictional.
-- Inventory model: one physical stock item equals one actual in-game object.
-- Customer model: customers are physically present NPCs who spawn from mall paths, may enter, browse, queue, buy, or leave.
-- Pricing model: suggested pricing for used goods; new goods use fixed contract pricing while considered new.
-- Layout model: customization is available immediately.
-- Documentation model: Markdown in repo is the production source of truth.
-- Visual gate: the first 0.3% store experience must reach visual sign-off before new gameplay breadth is added.
+After successful proof and explicit feedback, define one small retail encounter using that same visual treatment. No detailed scenery, full store, new simulation systems, or release work is part of the immediate milestone.
 
